# Build context exclusions for backend/Dockerfile and backend/Dockerfile.prod.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# Both Dockerfiles do `COPY . .`. Without a .dockerignore that copied the whole
# backend directory into the runtime image, including tracked SQLite databases
# holding bcrypt password hashes and ad-hoc scripts holding a live API key.
# Untracking a file from git does NOT remove it from the build context — only
# this file does. Keep the two in sync.

# NOTE ON PATTERNS: docker matches these against the path relative to the build
# context, so `*.db` covers only the context root. Anything that must match at
# any depth needs an explicit `**/` prefix.

# ── Credentials and local databases ──
**/*.db
**/*.db.old
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key

# ── Developer tool installs (never belong in a runtime image) ──
# `pip install --user` puts packages here, and `COPY . .` then bakes the whole
# lot into the image: a Trivy scan of the built image found pip-audit, ruff,
# cyclonedx and their dependency trees shipped to production. They are not
# imported by the app, so they are pure image bloat and extra attack surface —
# and Trivy reports their CVEs against the running image forever.
.local/

# ── Tests and dev-only tooling (not needed at runtime) ──
# Excluding tests/ from the image is also a safety control: on 2026-07-28 the
# suite was run via `docker exec ems_backend pytest` and, picking up the
# container's own DATABASE_URL, wrote fake providers/crew/PRFs into the live
# database. tests/conftest.py now refuses a non-local database; keeping the
# suite out of the runtime image removes the opportunity entirely.
tests/
pytest.ini
ruff.toml
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/

# ── Python build artefacts ──
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/

# ── Local data that must never be baked into an image ──
uploads/
logs/
**/*.log
global_errors.txt

# ── Scratch, one-off and diagnostic scripts ──
# These are developer tools run against a live system by hand; they have no
# business inside a production image.
add_*.py
fix_*.py
tmp_*.py
migrate_*.py
script_*.py
scratch_*.py
unfix_*.py
patch_*.py
probe.py
debug_*.py
*_check.py
*_scorecard.py
golden_claims_replay.py
seed_loadtest_data.py
